  <tei:standOff type="notes">
    <tei:note type="commentary" target="#note_1" xml:lang="en">
      <tei:p>God’s might is as much manifested in causing the rain as it is in reviving the dead. In fact the Talmud (Maseches Taanis 7a) declares in the name of Rabbi Avohu: “The day when rain falls is greater than the day of the resurrection of the dead.” Rabbi Yochanan said, (<tei:hi rend="italic">Ibid,</tei:hi> 2a): “There are three ׳keys’ which God retains in His own hands and does not entrust to any emissary: the key to rain, the key to childbirth, and the key to the resurrection of the dead.”</tei:p>
    </tei:note>
    <tei:note type="commentary" target="#note_2" xml:lang="en">
      <tei:p>God’s might is as much manifested in causing the rain as it is in reviving the dead. In fact the Talmud (Maseches Taanis 7a) declares in the name of Rabbi Avohu: “The day when rain falls is greater than the day of the resurrection of the dead.” Rabbi Yochanan said, (<tei:hi rend="italic">Ibid,</tei:hi> 2a): “There are three 'keys' which God retains in His own hands and does not entrust to any emissary: the key to rain, the key to childbirth, and the key to the resurrection of the dead.”</tei:p>
    </tei:note>
  </tei:standOff>
